---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyProse from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyProse.astro";
import CaseStudySection from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudySection.astro";
import CaseStudyCards from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCards.astro";
import CaseStudyCard from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCard.astro";

import heroImage from "../../assets/images/knowledge-base/home.png";
import brandImage from "../../assets/images/knowledge-base/brand-colours.png";

const stages = [
	{
		title: "Shared documents",
		text: "Guides started life as a scattered collection of documents on Google Drive. They existed, but they were hard to find and easy to let go out of date.",
	},
	{
		title: "A docs site (2024)",
		text: "I rebuilt them as an Astro Starlight site, then added Decap CMS so a colleague could add, edit and remove articles. In practice the team found the GitHub sign-in confusing, the editor unintuitive, and the CMS connection occasionally failed. An early chatbot experiment didn't make it past the trial stage either.",
	},
	{
		title: "Self-hosted Outline (2026)",
		text: "In summer 2026 we moved to Outline, an open-source wiki with real-time editing and fast search, which staff could use without learning anything new.",
	},
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="Building the company knowledge base"
	pageTitle="Knowledge base case study - Will Jackson"
	description="How I moved Woven & Woods' company know-how from scattered documents to a self-hosted Outline knowledge base that staff sign into with their existing Google accounts."
	path="/work/knowledge-base/"
	lede="A self-hosted knowledge base that puts the company's know-how in one searchable place, from IT and the websites to showroom procedures and the brand, which staff sign into with the accounts they already have."
	facts={[
		{ label: "Client", value: "Woven & Woods, flooring showrooms in Twickenham and Richmond" },
		{ label: "Role", value: "Setup, structure, writing and hosting" },
		{ label: "Stack", value: "Outline, Docker, Dokploy, Hetzner, Google Workspace" },
		{ label: "Year", value: "2024 to present" },
	]}
	heroImage={heroImage}
	heroImageAlt="The knowledge base home page, with collections for IT, websites, business development, technical, admin and showrooms"
>
	<CaseStudyProse heading="The brief">
		<p>
			In a small, growing business, a lot of know-how lives in people's
			heads: how to set up a new starter's laptop, how to update the
			website, how the showrooms run day to day. Every question that only
			one person can answer slows someone else down.
		</p>
		<p>
			The team needed one place to find answers, that the people who know
			the processes could keep up to date themselves, without needing me
			to publish changes for them.
		</p>
	</CaseStudyProse>

	<CaseStudyProse heading="Three versions, learning from each">
		<ol class="space-y-4">
			{
				stages.map((stage, index) => (
					<li class="grid gap-1 sm:grid-cols-[2rem_1fr]">
						<span class="font-heading font-bold text-teal-900">
							{index + 1}.
						</span>
						<span>
							<strong class="text-neutral-950">{stage.title}.</strong>{" "}
							{stage.text}
						</span>
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ol>
	</CaseStudyProse>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="Organised around how the business works"
		image={brandImage}
		imageAlt="The brand colours guide in the knowledge base, with Pantone, RGB, CMYK and hex values"
	>
		<p>
			I structured the knowledge base into collections that match how the
			business runs (IT, websites, showrooms, admin, business development
			and technical) and wrote its guides. They cover staff onboarding and
			offboarding, two-factor sign-in, the CRM, updating the websites,
			showroom procedures and the brand guidelines.
		</p>
		<p>
			New tools come with their own guide. When I built the outlet shop
			and the new website, the instructions for listing products and
			editing pages went straight into the knowledge base.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudyCards>
		<CaseStudyCard heading="Sign in with the accounts they already have">
			<p>
				Staff sign in with their existing Google Workspace accounts, so
				nobody has another password to remember and there's no separate
				password-protection layer to maintain. Collections are limited by
				role, and the directors and commercial manager use it alongside
				me.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>

		<CaseStudyCard heading="One server, several tools">
			<p>
				Outline runs in Docker on a Hetzner VPS managed with Dokploy,
				which supports Outline out of the box. The same server also runs
				the product data converter and a Kutt URL shortener.
			</p>
			<p>
				I've also connected the knowledge base to Claude, as the first
				step towards an easy-to-use assistant that can answer the team's
				questions from their own documentation.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>
	</CaseStudyCards>
</CaseStudyLayout>
